/**
`#id` and `.class` — the shorthand forms.

An id and a class are what a tag is usually named by, so they have a form
shorter than an attribute: `div#main.card.shadow` is
`div[id="main", class="card shadow"]`. The id comes first, then the classes.

A name that is only letters, digits, `_` or `-` is written bare. Anything else
is quoted — a Tailwind variant such as `"group-hover:bg-black/60"` would
otherwise end at its `:`, and the `/` would open a comment.

A run of `.class` shorthands is one `class` attribute, not several: `div.a.b`
and `div[class="a b"]` compile to the same tag, which is what lets the
formatter write either one as the other.

The bracket form carries what the shorthand cannot say — a value that is not
plain text:

- a **computed** one, `[class=variant]` or `[id=item.slug]`, since there is no
  shorthand for an expression;
- a **dict of conditions**, `[class={card: true, muted: false}]`, which is how
  several classes are switched at once (see
  [class_attribute](class_attribute.gadx)).

`gad transpile page.html` writes the shorthand wherever a page's `id` and
`class` are plain text, which is nearly everywhere.
**/

@main
    div#main.card.shadow
        h1.title Hello
    a."group-hover:bg-black/60"."md:px-6" quoted names
    @var (variant = "wide")
    section[class=variant]
        p Computed class
    section[class={card: true, muted: false}]
        p Conditional classes
